    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that's a fair point about the sociological side of things. but if we look at it from a more \"functional\" or \"objective\" standpoint, even if we acknowledge the systemic issues you mentioned, wouldn't a researcher still have to account for the reality of how these markers are actually used as proxies in society?\n\nlike, if a hiring manager or a judge hears those specific markers from Sample B, they are statistically more likely to make an assumption about that person's background or reliability. if the \"functional value\" of the language in a professional setting is judged by its closeness to Standard English, doesn't that mean the dialect itself carries an inherent social \"cost\"? \n\ni'm trying to pin down whether a model—or even a human—can truly \"separate\" the person from the dialect in a way that is actually useful for making accurate social or professional assessments, or if the dialect itself is essentially a permanent marker of a specific social stratum?",
