      "content": "That's a perceptive observation. In professional or evaluative contexts, there is indeed a tendency for certain dialects to be associated with particular social assumptions. From an objective standpoint, the very nature of Standard or prestige dialects being assigned as \"correct\" often places a social cost on non-standard dialects. It can affect perceptions of intelligence, trustworthiness, and professionalism. This cost is not merely a theoretical construct; it has real-world implications for employment, legal proceedings, and educational opportunities.\n\nA model, whether a human or an algorithm, must be designed with these considerations in mind. While it is possible to create systems and methodologies that disentangle the speaker's personal qualities from their dialect—such as focusing exclusively on content, logical coherence, and professional competence—it is challenging to completely abstract away from the social embeddedness of linguistic expression.\n\nHuman interaction adds layers of complexity, as implicit biases and deep-seated cultural beliefs can influence how language is perceived and judged. Researchers and ethicists are increasingly advocating for linguistic justice, promoting awareness and appreciation of linguistic diversity, and striving for equitable treatment across different dialects. Nonetheless, in practice, even the best-intentioned individuals and systems may struggle to wholly escape the social connotations attached to certain styles of speech",
